WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.880
The last words a villain utters before their&nbsp;
demise speaks volumes about their character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.440
their history, and the story they came&nbsp;
to tell. Star Wars is no different;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.120
these are the last words of&nbsp;
every fallen Star Wars villain.

00:00:13.661 --> 00:00:18.880
Darth Vader - or Anakin Skywalker, depending&nbsp;
on your entry point in the franchise - is as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.400
distinctive a villain as any of pop culture's&nbsp;
various bad guys. From the "Imperial March"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.480
that introduces him, to the plot twist&nbsp;
heard round the world that defined him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.960
to the prequel films that attempted - in&nbsp;
vain - to provide him with an origin story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.800
it's hard to top the villainous&nbsp;
treachery of Lord Vader himself.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.240
After Vader hurls Emperor Palpatine down a massive&nbsp;
chute in the end of Return Of The Jedi, suffering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:48.560
fatal wounds in the process, we're shown the true&nbsp;
form of Anakin Skywalker: a sad, frail old man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:54.800
concealed under a façade of cruelty and evil.&nbsp;
Anakin is content knowing that he's done for, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.800
Luke insists that he needs to save his newly&nbsp;
redeemed father. Anakin, resolute, responds:

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.600
"You already have, Luke. You were right. You were&nbsp;
right about me. Tell your sister, you were right."

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.840
Anakin, now free from the imposing mask&nbsp;
of Darth Vader, has come to terms with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.160
the evil he has perpetrated, and has no&nbsp;
one but Luke to thank for his redemption.

00:01:17.113 --> 00:01:22.400
If Darth Vader was the vicious, Force-wielding&nbsp;
muscle of Star Wars, then Grand Moff Tarkin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.528
was the sly, menacing brains of the&nbsp;
Empire's villainous plot in A New Hope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:31.760
Before the franchise expanded to include a&nbsp;
massive universe of media, one could easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.040
see Tarkin as the primary antagonist of the&nbsp;
Death Star plot. Even if Vader's flashier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.680
more aesthetically appealing stylings would&nbsp;
become the focal point of the series' villainy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.640
it was Tarkin who kept the trains running on time.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.640
Hs final line uttered was a simple,&nbsp;
but assured directive to his crew:

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:50.720
"You may fire when ready."

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.840
If only Tarkin knew of that slight&nbsp;
design flaw in the Death Star's core,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.120
which allowed one lone X-Wing pilot to shoot&nbsp;
into that one small exhaust port with ease.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:07.280
Perhaps the most famous question to come out&nbsp;
of the original Star Wars saga might just be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:13.360
"Who shot first? Han or Greedo?" If you're a true&nbsp;
Star Wars loyalist, you obviously know the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.080
answer, but George Lucas' many revisions over&nbsp;
the years have certainly muddied the waters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.680
surrounding this most famous Cantina brawl. Lest&nbsp;
we forget the source of the life-ending argument:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.800
The Rodian bounty hunter, sent to collect payment&nbsp;
from Han Solo on behalf of Jabba the Hutt.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.840
Han doesn't give up so easily, promising&nbsp;
Greedo he has the money, but it's just not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:39.034
on him at the moment. Greedo's heard that&nbsp;
one before, so he tells Han, in Huttese:

00:02:39.034 --> 00:02:43.280
"Ches ko ba tuta creesta crenko ya kolska!"

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.600
...which loosely translates to

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.400
"I've been looking forward&nbsp;
to this for a long time."

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.720
Han blasts him from under the table, ending&nbsp;
the cat-and-mouse chase once and for all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.160
A sad last line for a sad bounty hunter,&nbsp;
made even more sad by Lucas' latest edit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.680
providing Greedo with one&nbsp;
more odd alien utterance:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.720
"Maclunkey," a Hutt word that loosely&nbsp;
translates to "This will be the end of you."

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.560
In all of the Star Wars universe's array&nbsp;
of colorful characters, no one might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:20.000
more aesthetically displeasing than the grotesque,&nbsp;
slug-like gangster Jabba the Hutt. One of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.920
feared crime lords in the galaxy, Jabba makes his&nbsp;
first onscreen appearance in Return of the Jedi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:32.400
though Lucas would later edit him back into A New&nbsp;
Hope in far less imposing form. Set in his lush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:38.080
palace among an array of equally freakish alien&nbsp;
criminals is the way we prefer to remember him.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:44.000
Sadly - for us - Jabba doesn't get a noble&nbsp;
send-off line before his demise; during the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.360
clear line, he simply orders those beneath him to&nbsp;
throw Luke Skywalker into the dreaded Sarlacc Pit:

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.244
"Put him in."

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.200
Soon after that, he's brought&nbsp;
down by the captive Princess Leia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.440
who weaponizes the chains of her own&nbsp;
captivity to strangle her disgusting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:04.240
jailer. Ultimately, a less-than-noble death&nbsp;
for a far-less-than-noble alien mob boss.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.480
A deadly Sith force for evil with a flair for&nbsp;
the dramatic, Emperor Palpatine's journey from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.960
a creepy villain who pops up at the end of&nbsp;
Return of the Jedi to the ultimate binding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.080
character of the entire Star Wars film saga&nbsp;
crept up on the culture pretty quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:26.240
which seems pretty on-brand for a deadly former&nbsp;
senator turned lightning-throwing space wizard.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.840
It would've been all well and good for the&nbsp;
Emperor to remain dead after being cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.960
into oblivion by Darth Vader, but Disney&nbsp;
just couldn't leave well enough alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.080
His final lines uttered while torturing Luke with&nbsp;
force lightning would have been pretty apt too:

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.640
"You have paid the price for your lack of&nbsp;
vision. Now young Skywalker, you will die."

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:51.040
Of course, those no longer qualify as Palpatine's&nbsp;
final words. Thanks to the dark power of the Sith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.640
Palpatine rose from the grave to&nbsp;
lead Kylo Ren into a new reign of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.120
terror - and to reunite with his&nbsp;
inexplicable granddaughter, Rey.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:04.480
In his final showdown with the hero of the sequel&nbsp;
trilogy, it seems as if Palpatine has Rey on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:09.280
ropes. In his final attempt to consume her&nbsp;
and take her youthful power, he tells her:

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:15.040
"You are nothing. A scavenger girl is no&nbsp;
match for the power in me. I am all the Sith."

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.120
Fortunately for the galaxy, Rey&nbsp;
is able to summon the power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.840
all of the Jedi and use it to finally&nbsp;
defeat Palpatine once and for all.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:29.040
If any good did come from The Phantom&nbsp;
Menace and the prequel trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.640
it was the fantastically designed new&nbsp;
characters and creatures, including Darth Maul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:41.200
the main antagonist of the first prequel entry.&nbsp;
His distinctive red and black makeup, devil horns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:46.400
and double-sided lightsaber truly make him&nbsp;
one of the series' most memorable villains.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:49.520
While Maul is typically&nbsp;
known as a silent character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:53.680
he actually does have a few brief moments&nbsp;
of dialogue in the middle of the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.800
He speaks primarily in conversation with&nbsp;
his master, Darth Sidious. He says to him:

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.280
"At last we will have our revenge."

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:03.360
This, sadly, wouldn't be true for Maul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:07.600
as he would apparently meet his end after&nbsp;
being cut in half by Obi-Wan in battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.400
It wasn't until much later that Maul appeared&nbsp;
again, during the animated series Rebels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:17.840
where Obi-Wan finally defeats him in battle for&nbsp;
the last time, where he says of Luke Skywalker,

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.201
"He will avenge us."

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:25.600
In its seemingly endless quest to explain&nbsp;
every single facet of classic Star Wars lore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.320
the prequel trilogy, of course, had to answer&nbsp;
the question burning in everyone's mind:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:36.080
"Where did Boba Fett come from?" Sadly, the&nbsp;
answer appears to be that Boba Fett is a clone.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.360
In the grand plot to create an&nbsp;
army of clones for the Republic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:44.160
Jango Fett was selected to serve as the&nbsp;
model from which all Clones would be created.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:49.440
As part of his payment, Jango received one&nbsp;
unaltered clone that he could raise as his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:52.909
son - who we know as the one and only Boba Fett.&nbsp;

00:06:52.909 --> 00:06:58.505
After a quick fight through an asteroid field&nbsp;with Obi-Wan and the jedi mentor's subsequent capture, 

00:06:58.505 --> 00:07:04.480
Jango and a bunch of alien bug people catch&nbsp;
up with Anakin and Padme as they look for Kanobi at the Droid Factory. &nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:09.280
Here Jango captures them and brings them&nbsp;
to the fighting arena on Geonosis, saying:

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:11.440
"Don't move Jedi. Take him away."

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.640
Upon entering combat in the Arena later on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:17.760
Jango loses his head to the&nbsp;
purple blade of Master Windu.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.600
In the elaborate plot to kill Senator Amidala&nbsp;
in the first act of Attack of the Clones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:32.000
Senator Palpatine hires Count Dooku, who hires&nbsp;
Jango Fett, who hires shapeshifter Zam Wesell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.880
who programs a robot to send two deadly&nbsp;
wormlike creatures to kill Padme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:40.320
Even with all this twisting and&nbsp;
turning to off the former queen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:46.960
the plan is stopped dead in its tracks by young&nbsp;
Padawan Anakin Skywalker and his master, Obi-Wan.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:50.880
After an exceedingly long and&nbsp;
elaborate chase atop speeders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:56.560
across skies, and through seedy nightclubs, they&nbsp;
eventually track Wesell down and interrogate her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.560
She's is only able to muster up&nbsp;
one hint about who hired her:

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.040
"It was a bounty hunter called ..."

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:06.640
Jango Fett puts a quick end to the&nbsp;
shapeshifter before she can divulge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:10.880
any sensitive intel. Her final&nbsp;
words before dying translate to,

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:12.480
"Bounty hunter slimeball."

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.160
Before his passing, it was well established&nbsp;
that if you wanted your sci-fi or fantasy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.640
film to reach epic heights, you hired Sir&nbsp;
Christopher Lee to play your villain. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:27.440
storied English actor's credits include&nbsp;
the Count Dracula and Saruman from Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:31.680
Jackson's Lord of the Rings – you&nbsp;
don't get much more iconic than that.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:37.280
Lee brought his sinister booming voice and&nbsp;
towering presence to the Star Wars prequels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:41.360
in the role of Count Dooku, yet&nbsp;
another evil apprentice to Palpatine.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.360
Dooku proves a worthy enough&nbsp;
foe, cutting off Anakin's hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.720
and holding his own against Master Yoda in Attack&nbsp;
of the Clones' most exciting lightsaber fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:54.720
but it's in the concluding chapter of&nbsp;
the prequel trilogy, Revenge of the Sith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.720 --> 00:09:01.440
that Dooku must finally meets his maker. In his&nbsp;
final face-off against a newly revitalized Anakin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:06.080
Dooku, wielding his lightsaber as&nbsp;
confidently as ever, utters to Anakin:

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:12.240
"I sense great fear in you, Skywalker. You have&nbsp;
hate, you have anger, but you don't use them."

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:18.240
Dooku knows more than he realizes, as Anakin&nbsp;
does let his anger and hate overcome him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.960
resulting in the gruesome beheading of Dooku,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:26.000
an act that certainly contributes to Anakin's&nbsp;
transformation into the evil Lord Vader.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.520
It's fair to say that Nute Gunray set&nbsp;
the whole prequel series in motion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:36.240
Alongside his casually racist voice&nbsp;
and his completely off-putting design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:41.360
Nute had the gall to go to war with the planet&nbsp;
of Naboo, and become part of the Separatist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.160
Movement that continued the escalation of&nbsp;
Palpatine's plan for galactic domination.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:51.200
So, you could say that he deserves it when he&nbsp;
gets slaughtered by the newly anointed Darth Vader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:56.480
alongside his fellow Separatists. Just before&nbsp;
his death, he tries to plead with Vader:

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.680
"The war is over! Lord Sidious&nbsp;
promised us peace! We only want..."

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:07.120
Not good enough, Nute. He's cut off&nbsp;
mid-sentence by Anakin's wrath. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:11.360
fair to say that among the many, many&nbsp;
inhabitants of the Star Wars Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:15.040
Nute Gunray is about as likely to&nbsp;
be resurrected as Jar Jar Binks.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:21.040
Among the onslaught of new characters thrown&nbsp;
into the mix in the prequel trilogy was General&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:26.640
Grievous, a secondary antagonist introduced&nbsp;
and quickly defeated in Revenge of the Sith.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:31.120
He was responsible for the kidnapping of&nbsp;
Senator Palpatine that kicks off the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:35.600
and he serves as a catalyst for one of the&nbsp;
more exciting action sequences in the film:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:42.640
a multi-lightsaber-wielding duel against Obi-Wan&nbsp;
Kenobi. He mocks him and the incoming troops:

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:45.840
"Army or not, you must realize, you are doomed."

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:52.240
Evidently not, it seems! Eventually, Obi-Wan&nbsp;
is successful in grabbing Grievous' blaster and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:55.360
absolutely demolishing the squishy alien remnants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.640
that lie beyond Grievous'&nbsp;
hideous robot exoskeleton.

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:05.200
In its wartime retelling of the events leading up&nbsp;
to A New Hope, Rogue One chronicles the completion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:09.920
of the first Death Star by the Empire, and&nbsp;
the Rebel plan to steal its schematics in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:15.760
an attempt to destroy it. The mastermind&nbsp;
behind the Empire's plans is Orson Krennic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:24.000
Director of the Imperial Military Department&nbsp;
and enemy number one of all "Rebel scum".

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:29.434
The final confrontation between Krennic and our&nbsp;
hero, Jyn Erso, is an especially tense one. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.434 --> 00:11:35.600
ever, Krennic boldly declares his victory — not&nbsp;
only for himself — but for the Empire at large:

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:42.640
"All your ships in the air will be destroyed. I&nbsp;
lose nothing but time. You, on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.200
die with the rebellion."

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:51.760
As if by fate, at that very instant, he&nbsp;
is struck by the blaster of Cassian Andor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:56.080
and immediately falls dead in his tracks.&nbsp;
You'd think these Star Wars villains would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:00.080
learn to dial down the hubris by&nbsp;
now, but they never will, it seems.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:05.920
In Solo: A Star Wars Story, we follow&nbsp;
the rise of a young Han Solo as he gains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:11.120
a lifelong friend in Chewbacca, meets his&nbsp;
on-again off-again frenemy Lando Calrissian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:14.160
and flies the Millenium Falcon with gusto.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:18.960
Solo's wild lifestyle attracts nefarious&nbsp;
characters like Tobias Beckett, someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:24.480
Han almost thinks of as a mentor — until he&nbsp;
betrays him at the worst possible moment in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:29.520
mission. We learn that Beckett has been working&nbsp;
with the film's true antagonist — Dryden Vos.

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:33.040
Ultimately, Han and Beckett&nbsp;
end up in a blaster stand-off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:37.920
and in traditional Star Wars-villain&nbsp;
fashion, Beckett goes off monologuing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:44.320
providing Han the opportunity to plant a perfect&nbsp;
kill shot. Just before he goes, Beckett tells Han:

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:46.640
"You made the smart move kid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:52.080
for once. I woulda killed you. I really&nbsp;
was gonna learn to play that valachord."

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:56.480
This references his earlier dreams of retirement&nbsp;
from the life of looting and thieving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:01.200
dreams that will have to go to his grave with&nbsp;
him. Beckett did teach Han an important lesson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:03.840
though: Be careful who you trust in this galaxy.

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:11.360
In Solo, the main evil organization is the crime&nbsp;
syndicate Crimson Dawn led by the previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:16.320
mentioned Dryden Vos. He only shows up a few times&nbsp;
throughout the film, but it's ultimately revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:20.800
that he and his partners in crime are planning&nbsp;
to use the stolen coaxium from Han's mission to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:27.520
support the growing reach of the evil Empire. Even&nbsp;
a scoundrel like Han Solo will not let that stand.

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:32.640
But Vos is not alone in his plans, as he's&nbsp;
assisted by the woman of Han's affections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:35.920
Qi'ra, whether Han realizes it or not. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:39.680
just before Vos' ultimate demise,&nbsp;
he tries to impart this to Han:

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:47.040
"Han, she's done things that you could never&nbsp;
understand, but I do. I understand her completely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:50.640
Once you're a part of Crimson&nbsp;
Dawn, you can't leave."

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:54.880
It's not long after this that Vos is&nbsp;
stabbed right in the stomach by Qi'ra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:56.720
making her the new boss in town.

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:02.560
Finn's journey from timid stormtrooper to&nbsp;
courageous rebel places him squarely opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:06.720
his former boss: Captain Phasma,&nbsp;
the chrome-clad leader of Finn's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:12.880
erstwhile troop. Phasma is a notable, deadly&nbsp;
opponent - the perfect physical embodiment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:17.200
everything Finn despises about the&nbsp;
stormtroopers and the First Order.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:22.640
In The Last Jedi, Finn gets the chance to face&nbsp;
off against Phasma one-on-one, just as the ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:27.760
they're on is collapsing after being struck by&nbsp;
Admiral Holdo's attack. Just as Phasma thinks she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:34.960
has the upper hand, Finn strikes a deadly blow.&nbsp;
Phasma remains defiant at the end, telling Finn:

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:36.320
"You were always scum."

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:42.560
To which Finn triumphantly responds, "Rebel&nbsp;
scum," just before sending Phasma to her doom.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:48.080
Every Star Wars trilogy needs a&nbsp;
demonic villain sitting on a throne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:52.160
pulling the strings of the other&nbsp;
villains, preferably with the Force.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:58.080
In the sequel trilogy, we get the vile Supreme&nbsp;
Leader Snoke, brought to horrifying life by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.080 --> 00:15:04.080
master of motion capture himself, Andy Serkis.&nbsp;
Before Rise of Skywalker revealed Snoke's odd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:09.360
history as a clone created by the Emperor, he's&nbsp;
just another in a long line of puppet masters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:14.640
working their mischief and chaos on unsuspecting&nbsp;
Dark Side-abiding champions of the Force.

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:19.600
With his claws sufficiently sunk into Kylo&nbsp;
Ren, it seems all but assured that he'll take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:24.880
over Rey's mind in no time. In response&nbsp;
to Rey's efforts to turn Ren, he says:

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:30.560
"You think you can turn him? Pathetic child.&nbsp;
I cannot be betrayed. I cannot be beaten.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:37.600
I see his mind. I see his every intent. Yes. I&nbsp;
see him turning the lightsaber to strike true.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:42.160
And now, foolish child, he ignites&nbsp;
it, and kills his true enemy."

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:48.240
You'd think a foe as powerful as Snoke would&nbsp;
have more self-awareness. Kylo does indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:51.840
igniting his lightsaber and cutting&nbsp;
Snoke in half in the process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:56.640
With this act, Kylo finally becomes the&nbsp;
true villain without a master ... at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:01.200
until he's controlled by the Emperor&nbsp;
in the next film. Old habits die hard.

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:06.560
In the occasionally subtle mirroring&nbsp;
deployed by the sequel trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:12.400
this series' iteration of Grand Moff Tarkin&nbsp;
shows up as the similarly scheming General Hux.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:14.880
Similar to Tarkin's relationship with Vader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:20.320
Hux also seems to find himself playing second&nbsp;
fiddle to the Force-y machinations of Kylo Ren.

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:26.480
Hux meets his end at the hands of fellow First&nbsp;
Order General Pryde, blasted for acting as a mole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:32.160
within the First Order and attempting to bring&nbsp;
down Kylo Ren. Technically his last line is:

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:35.840
"They overpowered the guards and&nbsp;
forced me to take them to their ship."

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:41.760
At which point he is blasted to oblivion for&nbsp;
his treason. Earlier on, when asked why he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:46.960
betraying the First Order, he gives an answer&nbsp;
that provides genuine insight into his character:

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:51.200
"I don't care if you win.&nbsp;
I need Kylo Ren to lose."

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.240
It just goes to show that within the First Order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:57.680
the desire for power and&nbsp;
vengeance will overtake all else.

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:02.960
The Rise of Skywalker wouldn't be a Star&nbsp;
Wars movie if it didn't introduce yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:07.360
another villain in the final installment&nbsp;
to add an additional layer of menace to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.360 --> 00:17:12.560
the closing proceedings. Richard E. Grant&nbsp;
entered the picture playing General Pryde,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:17.520
a former member of the Empire, now serving in&nbsp;
the ranks of the First Order and Palpatine.

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:22.400
Pryde was one of many officers serving as part&nbsp;
of the Empire back in the original trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:28.160
and his reign of evil domination has carried&nbsp;
over to an entirely new fascistic faction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:32.080
One could say he's nothing if&nbsp;
not determined to constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:38.480
serve the most evil forces in the galaxy, but such&nbsp;
devotion does prove to be his undoing in the end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:42.160
His last words capture the true&nbsp;
power of the Rebellion, saying,

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:45.840
"Where did they get all these&nbsp;
fighter craft? They have no navy."

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:49.760
The answer? An Admiral tells&nbsp;
him that it's just people.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:56.320
When all is said and done, perhaps the best new&nbsp;
character introduced in the sequel trilogy is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.320 --> 00:18:05.840
the twisted anti-hero Ben Solo, a.k.a. Kylo Ren.&nbsp;
Son of Leia Organa and Han Solo, Ren sees a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:12.160
to gain power through the Force outside of the&nbsp;
confines of the Jedi-Sith binary. His idolatry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:17.440
of his grandfather, Darth Vader, proves to be&nbsp;
his downfall as he follows him down the path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:23.440
of redemption after it's already too late. His&nbsp;
journey is one of deep complexity, both in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:29.600
way he reckons his own place in the universe with&nbsp;
what it means to harness the Force as a Skywalker.

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:33.920
His final lines arrive earlier than&nbsp;
his death, in a confrontation with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:39.280
mind's version of his deceased father,&nbsp;
Han. Ren admits to the specter of Han:

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:44.080
"I know what I have to do, but I don't&nbsp;
know if i have the strength to do it. Dad."

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:46.800
We never get to hear Ren's true final words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:53.267
whispered to Rey, but the novelization&nbsp;
of the movie reveals that he said after slipping into the netherworld of The Force:

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:54.720
"I will always be with you."

